istories: Previous player played F2 earlier (in history), next player didn't play F2, across player played F2. So chances to get another F2 are low.  \n\n**Possible chow formations:**  \n- W4, W7, W8: can make Chow W7-W8-W9 or W4-W5-W6 or W6-W7-W8 etc. But W6 I have none. W9 none.  \n- T2,T3,T4: can make Chow T2-T3-T4 (I have T2\u00d71, T3\u00d72, T4\u00d71). That's almost a Chow already if I get T1 or T5 for different chow combos? Actually T2,T3,T4 is itself a Chow! Wait, I have T2=1, T3=2, T4=1 \u2192 that\u2019s T2,T3,T4, and T3,T4,Tx? No, the T3 duplicates mean one T3 can be part of Chow T2,T3,T4 and another T3 can try for another Chow.  \n\nSo currently hidden meld in hand: I have potential Chow T2,T3,T4 (using 1 T2, 1 T3, 1 T4). And leftover tiles: W4\u00d72, W7, W8, T3\u00d71 leftover, B6\u00d72, F2.\n\nWhat is good discard?  \nDiscard honors first if not useful: F2 is seat wind but only 1 left to complete pung, maybe keep for chance? But that reduces flexibility.  \n\nBetter to aim for hand with All Simples + maybe Mixed Triple Chow? No.  \n\nCheck tile safety:  \nPrevious player's meld: W7,W8,W9 (Chow) and F1 pung, so they may need W6 or W9 etc but W7/W8 they already used. Next player's melds: B6,B7,B8 Chow, B5 pung, T7 pung \u2192 they have many simple tiles. Across player: W1,W2,W3 Chow.  \n\nSo discarding terminals or honors might be safer:  \n- F2 is relatively safe because many winds are out (F1 pung, F4 none left, F3 played multiple times).  \n- W7/W8 are relatively dangerous because previous player might need them for pair? They already have W7,W8,W9 Chow, so maybe they don't need W7/W8 now.  \n\nBut my hand is far from winning; I should discard tiles that are less useful for forming melds.\n\nMy best meld potential:  \n1. Keep T2,T3,T4 to form Chow.  \n2. Keep W4\u00d72 for potential pung or chow W3-W4-W5 or W4-W5-W6 (W5 unshown count 3, W6 4).  \n3. W7,W8 can pair or chow with W6/W9.  \n4. B6\u00d72 can pung if I get one more B6 (unshown B6:3 left)